  {
    "episode_id": 70674,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/0/70674.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "It's great to be with you, too. And thanks for your voice and for all that you do. And I'm honored to be here with you today. But listen, those are some good questions you put out there, who I am, where I came from. Why am I doing this? I have those questions a lot. Really, my background is that a ministry. I was a pastor for almost 30 years prior to going to Congress. And quite frankly, never was it on my radar to go to Congress or Any of political office for that matter I of course had helped a number of people get elected over the course of my time in ministry But never thought that I would be one of those individuals and basically what happened we had a couple of battles that came our way in our church in Barrow County Georgia and both of those battles took on national attention and kind of threw me out of the pulpit, if you will, and on a more national scale. And one thing led to another, but the bottom line, one of those battles involved the ACLU. They sued our county over the 10 commandments in the courthouse and just have to be our church and put those county those commandments in the courthouse and so very literally that ACLU lawsuit marched right down the halls of our church family we had a number of county commissioners and so forth and so that was one of the battles I went before the county commission and I said look if you'll fight the ACLU then I'll pay for it so it doesn't cost the taxpayers a Barrow County and they agreed to do so and there's a life transforming time in my life we literally fought for three years We ended up losing that battle. The Ten Commandments had to come down. And at that point, Rick, we started focusing our attention on the state. And for another six years, we dealt with the state legislature. And I was very honored in 2012 to be in Governor Dill's office when he signed a new law that any nine document, there's a nine document historical display that includes the Ten Commandments, but it's basically the moral foundation of American law and justice. And that nine document display is legal in any government building in the state of Georgia. So that went right back up in Barrow County. In fact, that display is in the capital in Atlanta. And counties all across the state. But That was one of the battles the other involved the IRS and their attempts to censor what can be said in churches and I became one of 33 pastors to challenge the IRS across the country and so both of those battles just came our way and both of them kind of pushed me out of the pulpit if you will into the public arena and one thing led to another. and our representative retired and we started having people say, listen, Jody, would you consider running for Congress? And one thing led to another and here we are.",
