  {
    "episode_id": 70674,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/0/70674.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "Well I know we had when they gave in to Stacy and her group, they gave in to a lot of stuff that really killed us that we did not have to do. I mean the piling up of the boxes, the drop-offs that were in a 24-hour day drop-off boxes and unsupervised, there's just so many things that didn't have to happen that COVID had no effect on or anything else. People could still vote. I've been through all the way through this. I have not changed my life one iota. I voted. I voted in public. I mean, it's just and I've never had COVID knock on wood. And I may get it. And you know, if I do, if I don't, I don't. It's like the flu. Luckily, I don't get it. But, you know, there's a lot of things that happened in that just didn't need to happen. You're exactly right. And not all of it is, you know, most of it is stuff that he could have controlled. After the election fact, that's another issue we deal with at another time. But what actually happened coming up to the election, he very much had, say so in, and how it was played out. Well, you were the first statewide candidate in Georgia that was endorsed by President Trump. Do you think that that endorsement has really helped you in your campaign?",
